    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle, we need to first understand the properties of the angles in a triangle.\n\n1. **Sum of Interior Angles**: The sum of the interior angles in any triangle is always 180 degrees. This is a fundamental property of triangles.\n\n2. **Mean Value**: The mean value of a set of numbers is calculated by summing all the numbers and then dividing by the count of the numbers.\n\n3. **Application to Angles**: Here, we have three angles in a triangle, and we need to find their mean value.\n\nLet's denote the three angles of the triangle as \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\).\n\n- The sum of these angles is given by:\n  \\[\n  A + B + C = 180^\\circ\n  \\]\n\n4. **Calculating the Mean**:\n  The mean value of the three angles \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) is calculated by dividing the sum of the angles by the number of angles (which is 3).\n\n  \\[\n  \\text{Mean} = \\frac{A + B + C}{3}\n  \\]\n\n5. **Substituting the Sum**:\n  Substitute the sum of the angles (180 degrees) into the equation:\n\n  \\[\n  \\text{Mean} = \\frac{180^\\circ}{3}\n  \\]\n\n6. **Performing the Division**:\n  \\[\n  \\text{Mean} = 60^\\circ\n  \\]\n\nTherefore, the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle is \\(\\boxed{60^\\circ}\\).",
